WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.649
Sony Pictures lifted the review embargo for
its highly-anticipated anti-hero flick Venom

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:09.930
on Tuesday, October 2nd, ahead of its October
5th release date, granting permission to the

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:14.450
fortunate few who saw the film at its world
premiere in Los Angeles to post their spoiler-free

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:14.940
thoughts.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.660
In other words, the reviews are in!

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:17.980
And they're weird.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.759
Bottom line: Many critics thought it was hilarious,
but not quite on purpose, and that it features

00:00:22.759 --> 00:00:27.859
an unwelcome throwback feel, as if the MCU
comic book movie renaissance never happened.

00:00:27.859 --> 00:00:32.009
The Hollywood Reporter's Todd McCarthy, for
example, thinks that Venom is more black sheep

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:33.620
than Black Panther, writing:

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.980
"Venom feels like a throwback, a poor second
cousin to the all-stars that have reliably

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.940
dominated the box-office charts for most of
this century."

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.380
There's a silver lining, however: Tom Hardy.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.720
Collider's Perri Nemiroff found Hardy typically
charming as Eddie Brock, but wished the film

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:49.480
had more of an edge, writing,

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.400
"Significant chunks of Venom don't work at
all but there is some serious charm to the

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:54.900
Eddie/Venom relationship.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:59.430
Not sure I had the intended reactions to some
scenes but fun is fun even when it's totally

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.440
ridiculous, right?

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.620
It's too bad they didn't go for the R rating
though."

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.680
Nerdist's Hector Navarro felt much the same,
but pointed out that the film kept true to

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:11.220
the source material in portraying Eddie as
a guy with an off-kilter sense of humor, writing,

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.990
"Despite some problems [Venom] did right by
its main character [...] People forget that

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:19.040
he's always had a weird sense of humor in
the comics, and that humor is fully on display

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:19.680
in the movie!"

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.600
The Verge's Bryan Bishop wrote that Hardy
gave a fully fleshed-out, "capital-P performance"

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.840
as Venom, writing,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.620
"Hardy is always watchable, no matter the
role, but there's so much to take in here

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:32.170
that it almost feels like he's putting on
a one-man show."

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:37.490
Echoing that sentiment, Uproxx senior entertainment
writer Mike Ryan said he was, quote, "fascinated

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:41.460
with Venom," mostly because while the rest
of the cast was in a dark superhero movie,

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.120
Hardy was decidedly not.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:43.940
Ryan tweeted,

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.800
"[Hardy is] basically remaking Jim Carrey's
Liar Liar.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.260
I kinda loved watching this movie, in a Rocky
Horror type of way."

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.160
Oh, I have a parasite.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.120
Good night, Mrs. Chen!"

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.380
One of the harshest reactions to Venom came
from IGN's Tom Jorgensen, who wrote,

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:05.360
"Sorry to say that Venom is pretty much a
complete failure a tonal mess that feels 15

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.840
years old, ignoring the storytelling strides
that the superhero genre has made in recent

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:10.340
years."

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:15.700
MCU Cosmic's Jeremy Conrad kept his critique
simple but biting, sharing a tweet that references

00:02:15.709 --> 00:02:18.630
part of a line Venom snarls in one of the
film's trailers.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:19.910
"Like a turd.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:20.960
In the wind."

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.120
Freelance comics journalist Meg Downey delivered
perhaps the most interesting response to Venom,

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:31.400
calling it an "excruciatingly surreal experience"
and likening it to Ben Affleck's Daredevil,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.340
Ang Lee's Hulk, and Halle Berry's Catwoman
not exactly great company.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:36.640
Downey wrote in one tweet,

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:41.020
"The humor, the story beats, everything right
down to the Eminem theme song feels like it

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.880
emerged Kimmy Schmidt-style from a sealed
off early-'00s bunker."

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:49.600
All in all, the reviews indicate that viewer
mileage will vary wildly with Venom, putting

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.360
it just ahead of other franchise non-starters
like the disastrous Universal outing The Mummy.

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.620
But Owen Gleiberman of Variety thinks it might
not be enough:

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:01.959
"This gateway into the Sony Universe of Marvel
Characters […] may not sputter as badly

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:06.060
as Tom Cruise's The Mummy, but it could turn
out to be a similar case of a franchise kickoff

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.580
that doesn't fully attain franchise liftoff."

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.840
But if it does well enough at the box office,
the planned Venom trilogy could indeed become

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:17.590
a reality, and all signs indicate the franchise
is primed to improve with each installment,

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:20.780
leading up to fingers crossed a potential
Spider-Man crossover.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:26.080
Or at least a substantial appearance by Venom's
rival, Carnage, if and spoiler alert! those

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.780
early reports about the mid-credits scene
prove correct.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.760
Make your own call October 5th, when Venom
hits theaters nationwide.

